  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 PM EDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nStrong upper-level ridging persists this afternoon with surface\nhigh pressured noted over the Northeast. As a result, status-\nquo on the forecast overall including cruise-control \ntemperatures within a couple of degrees these next two days. \nWe'll be monitoring for fog tonight into early Monday morning. \nGuidance appears most keen on the I-69 corridor including \nnorthwest Ohio, yet light surface wind from the south may limit \nany Lake Erie moisture advection, while radiational cooling \nappears to be sufficient. \n\nLow pressure over Manitoba this afternoon features a cold front \ndraped across the Central and Northern Plains. This front attempts to \nseep over our area Tuesday as it gets hung-up atop our persistent \nupper-level ridge. This will knock temperatures down a few degrees \nTuesday, yet temperatures will still be well above normal (normal \nhigh/low 60/40). Otherwise, remaining very mild and dry this week \nwith our next chance for any rain not arriving until perhaps \nThursday afternoon (20% chance) as another front attempts to cross \nour ridge. The abundance of dry air and overall absence of forcing \nleaves me unimpressed. Instead, late next weekend into early \nNovember looks to be the better opportunity to end our dry spell \nwith low pressure emerging from the southern/central Plains.",
